// Correctness of reliable broadcast, keyed per node (Blossom golden corpus, derived = true; see ../../README.md).
//
// LDFI (SIGMOD 2015) Figure 3 states the property as "if a correct process has a log entry, all correct processes
// have it", with post = "my log entry exists and no node is missing it". Molly's delivery spec additionally excludes
// the broadcaster from witnessing pre (it holds the entry from time 1 whatever happens; R06 §2.4). This file states
// the same oracle with the negation moved out of post:
//
//   pre(N, P):  some correct node M other than the broadcaster, with N as a neighbour, holds P;
//   post(N, P): N holds P.
//
// On the fully connected group of group.ded both formulations judge every run alike (the Molly oracle, TEST-022, reads
// them at EOT; every node holds P at EOT in every failure-free run of this family):
//   Molly:  bad  <=>  exists X correct, X != broadcaster, holding P  and  exists A not holding P;
//   here:   bad  <=>  exists N not holding P  and  exists M correct, M != broadcaster, holding P, node(M, N)
//                 <=>  the same condition, because node(M, N) holds for every M != N and M holds P while N does not.
// Keeping post monotone means the outcome lineage is exactly the lineage of `log`, which is what the paper's game
// (§3, Figures 4-7) reasons about, and conservative negative support (CR-31) has nothing to add for the negation-free
// protocols of the family. With `notin missing_log` in post, every log fact at every earlier time becomes a possible
// cause of the negated goal, and the hypotheses grow far beyond the published run counts (Figures 12-13, BENCH-136),
// which a search without negative support reproduces exactly on this family.
// A case with Figure 3 verbatim (fig3_spec.ded) keeps that negative-support path under test.

pre(N, P) :- log(M, P), node(M, N), notin bcast(M, P)@1, notin crash(M, M, _);
post(N, P) :- log(N, P);
